Applies `highlights` over the inherited text style of the element.

Each highlight is a byte range and a [`HighlightStyle`]. The inherited style
is read from the window when the element is laid out, so a highlight changes
only the properties it sets. Text outside every range keeps the inherited style.

The ranges must be sorted by start, must not overlap, and must lie on
character boundaries. Debug builds check that each range is inside the text.
Release builds don't check, and unsorted ranges give wrong layouts or panic.

# Examples

```
# use gpui::*;
# fn render() -> impl IntoElement {
StyledText::new("Press Enter to save").with_highlights([(
    6..11,
    HighlightStyle {
        font_weight: Some(FontWeight::BOLD),
        ..Default::default()
    },
)])
# }
```

Creates a subscription that runs `unsubscribe` when it's dropped.

Use it to give your own resource the same drop-to-cancel behavior as the subscriptions that GPUI
returns.

# Examples

```
# use gpui::Subscription;
# use std::{cell::Cell, rc::Rc};
let cleaned_up = Rc::new(Cell::new(false));

let subscription = Subscription::new({
    let cleaned_up = cleaned_up.clone();
    move || cleaned_up.set(true)
});
assert!(!cleaned_up.get());

drop(subscription);
assert!(cleaned_up.get());
```

Parses and rasterizes `bytes` at a logical `scale_factor`, in one call.

This is [`SvgRenderer::parse_svg`] followed by [`SvgRenderer::render_parsed`] with
[`SvgSize::ScaleFactor`]. If you need the same SVG at several sizes, parse it once and call
`render_parsed` for each size.

# Errors

Returns a `usvg::Error` if `bytes` is not a valid SVG, or if the rasterized size is zero.

# Examples

```
# use gpui::*;
# use std::sync::Arc;
let renderer = SvgRenderer::new(Arc::new(AssetRegistry::default()));
let svg = br#"<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="12"></svg>"#;
let image = renderer.render_single_frame(svg, 1.0).unwrap();

assert_eq!(image.frame_count(), 1);
```
